{"page":734,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.36456,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"720 Chapter 12: Vectors and the Geometry of Space"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.09317,"w":0.55871,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"EXAMPLE 2 Find the angle between u = i - 2j - 2k and v = 6i + 3j + 2k."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.12087,"w":0.24992,"h":0.01365,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution We use the formula above:"},{"x":0.39095,"y":0.13959,"w":0.37952,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"u # v = (1)(6) + (-2)(3) + (-2)(2) = 6 - 6 - 4 = -4"},{"x":0.40075,"y":0.15851,"w":0.28712,"h":0.02588,"fs":0.01353,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"0 u0 = 2(1)2 + (-2)2 + (-2)2 = 29 = 3"},{"x":0.40169,"y":0.18153,"w":0.2718,"h":0.02588,"fs":0.01353,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"0 v0 = 2(6)2 + (3)2 + (2)2 = 249 = 7"},{"x":0.41129,"y":0.20913,"w":0.11327,"h":0.02898,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"u = cos-1 a u # v"},{"x":0.49255,"y":0.2104,"w":0.35288,"h":0.03727,"fs":0.01353,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"0 u0 0 v0 b = cos-1 a -4 (3)(7)b ≈1.76 radians or 100.98°."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.27579,"w":0.59301,"h":0.02941,"fs":0.01918,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The angle formula applies to two-dimensional vectors as well. Note that the angle u is acute if u # v 7 0 and obtuse if u # v 6 0."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.32848,"w":0.59299,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 3 Find the angle u in the triangle ABC determined by the vertices"},{"x":0.12493,"y":0.32993,"w":0.03634,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"B(3, 5)"},{"x":0.32498,"y":0.34485,"w":0.3592,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"A = (0, 0), B = (3, 5), and C = (5, 2) (Figure 12.22)."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.36532,"w":0.59303,"h":0.03521,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"Solution The angle u is the angle between the vectors r CA and r CB. The component forms of these two vectors are"},{"x":0.18296,"y":0.37889,"w":0.03708,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"C(5, 2)"},{"x":0.46435,"y":0.40416,"w":0.30687,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"r CA = 8-5, -29 and r CB = 8-2, 39."},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.43941,"w":0.20241,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 12.22 The triangle in Example 3."},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.44145,"w":0.46484,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"First we calculate the dot product and magnitudes of these two vectors."},{"x":0.4938,"y":0.45932,"w":0.24466,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"r CA # r CB = (-5)(-2) + (-2)(3) = 4"},{"x":0.51667,"y":0.47824,"w":0.22593,"h":0.02588,"fs":0.01353,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"0 r CA 0 = 2(-5)2 + (-2)2 = 229"},{"x":0.51667,"y":0.50126,"w":0.21466,"h":0.02588,"fs":0.01353,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"0 r CB 0 = 2(-2)2 + (3)2 = 213"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.54121,"w":0.27611,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Then applying the angle formula, we have"},{"x":0.50309,"y":0.54949,"w":0.07792,"h":0.04671,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"u = cos-1 ¢"},{"x":0.58519,"y":0.56714,"w":0.06073,"h":0.04068,"fs":0.01918,"n":6,"k":0,"t":"r CA # r CB 0 r CA 0 0 r CB 0"},{"x":0.51831,"y":0.59169,"w":0.17329,"h":0.05756,"fs":0.01441,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"= cos-1 ¢ 4 1 22921 2132 ≤"},{"x":0.51632,"y":0.65946,"w":0.16882,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"≈78.1° or 1.36 radians."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.69943,"w":0.14345,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Orthogonal Vectors"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.71365,"w":0.59301,"h":0.06927,"fs":0.01918,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Two nonzero vectors u and v are perpendicular if the angle between them is p>2. For such vectors, we have u # v = 0 because cos (p>2) = 0. The converse is also true. If u and v are nonzero vectors with u # v = 0 u0 0 v0 cos u = 0, then cos u = 0 and u = cos-1 0 = p>2. The following definition also allows for one or both of the vectors to be the zero vector."},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.80756,"w":0.37758,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"DEFINITION Vectors u and v are orthogonal if u # v = 0."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.86348,"w":0.58403,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 4 To determine if two vectors are orthogonal, calculate their dot product."},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.87776,"w":0.58814,"h":0.04301,"fs":0.01918,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"(a) u = 83, -29 and v = 84, 69 are orthogonal because u # v = (3)(4) + (-2)(6) = 0. (b) u = 3i - 2j + k and v = 2j + 4k are orthogonal because u # v = (3)(0) +"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.92205,"w":0.14848,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(-2)(2) + (1)(4) = 0."}]}